t of group work, bouncing ideas off peers, and tackling massive problems together. Because the work is challenging, math majors often form incredibly tight-knit, supportive friend groups. He won't be isolated; he’ll be part of a team. **5. "Nerd" is no longer a bad word.** Pop culture has shifted massively in the last decade. Being deeply passionate and knowledgeable about a complex subject is now widely celebrated. The people who are considered "nerds" in high school are often the ones running the world, building the coolest technology, and living very comfortable lives in their twenties and thirties. **Actionable advice for him:** Tell him to take a few introductory math classes during his freshman year, along with some electives in other areas (like computer science, economics, or even history). He doesn't have to declare his major on day one. If he finds that he loves the math, enjoys the professors, and gets along with the students, he'll know he's in the right place. If not, he can easily pivot to something else!
